1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:04,769
Dispatch brings calls, apparatus,
personnel, a map, and reference

2
00:00:04,769 --> 00:00:06,429
notes into one workspace.

3
00:00:06,889 --> 00:00:11,229
This lesson follows a read-only reference
check in a fictional training department.

4
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:14,670
From Home, open the menu
in the upper-left corner.

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:20,340
Map, POIs, Protocols, and Contacts
lead to different kinds of information.

6
00:00:20,809 --> 00:00:23,719
Choose the reference that answers
your question before changing

7
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:25,620
a call or assigning resources.

8
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,450
We will use the map and site
records without making a dispatch.

9
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:32,780
Open Map for a larger view
of the Chicago training area.

10
00:00:33,189 --> 00:00:36,730
The folder control in the
lower-left corner opens Map Layers.

11
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:39,549
Academy Staging is
enabled in this example.

12
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:44,150
Choose Hide All and inspect the
changed layer switch, then close

13
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:45,549
the panel to compare the map.

14
00:00:45,909 --> 00:00:48,950
The blue staging point disappears
while the other incident

15
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:50,309
and unit markers remain.

16
00:00:50,729 --> 00:00:54,159
Reopen the panel and choose
Show All to restore the layer.

17
00:00:54,499 --> 00:00:58,210
These controls change what this
map displays; they do not delete

18
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:00,020
the point or dispatch a resource.

19
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,630
For a named reference,
choose POIs from the menu.

20
00:01:04,199 --> 00:01:07,879
The list starts with All
types and Name, A to Z.

21
00:01:08,349 --> 00:01:11,689
Select Academy Staging in
the type selector, then

22
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,239
enter Staging in Search POIs.

23
00:01:14,649 --> 00:01:17,700
The existing Academy Staging
Point remains in the result.

24
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:22,719
A type filter and a search work together,
so check both when a point seems missing.

25
00:01:23,119 --> 00:01:27,770
This example has only one point; it does
not demonstrate a change in sorting order.

26
00:01:27,970 --> 00:01:30,219
Read the card before using its location.

27
00:01:30,539 --> 00:01:35,999
It identifies Academy Staging Point, its
Academy Staging type, a Destination badge,

28
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,199
and a clearly marked training address.

29
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,600
The note calls it a fictional exercise
assembly point and says it provides

30
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,909
no real deployment or access approval.

31
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:48,930
Use those stored details to confirm
you found the intended reference.

32
00:01:49,309 --> 00:01:53,280
A point on this map is not evidence
that a device reported its position

33
00:01:53,430 --> 00:01:55,059
or that a crew reached the site.

34
00:01:55,239 --> 00:01:58,930
Protocols is a separate menu
entry for departmental procedures.

35
00:01:59,339 --> 00:02:03,569
Here the page says No protocols
found and explains that none have

36
00:02:03,569 --> 00:02:05,000
been created for the department.

37
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:08,199
That is the available
information for this exercise.

38
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:11,579
There is no procedure to open
or follow in this recording.

39
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:15,730
Treat an empty reference page as something
to resolve through your authorized

40
00:02:15,730 --> 00:02:20,300
departmental process, rather than assuming
the app supplied the missing instructions.

41
00:02:20,519 --> 00:02:22,670
Open Contacts to find the site record.

42
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:28,530
Enter Warehouse in Search contacts, then
open Training Warehouse, marked fictional.

43
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:33,669
Contact Details keeps that name and the
Company category above its reference tabs.

44
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,580
The Contact Information section
shows an email address and office

45
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,010
phone for this training record.

46
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:42,740
Confirm the identity before
reading further; a similar

47
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:44,389
place name is not enough.

48
00:02:44,799 --> 00:02:49,059
We inspect the stored reference here
without calling, emailing, opening an

49
00:02:49,059 --> 00:02:51,340
address link, or changing the contact.

50
00:02:51,469 --> 00:02:54,100
The supporting tabs stay
with the same contact.

51
00:02:54,549 --> 00:02:57,999
Open Notes: this warehouse
has no notes in that tab.

52
00:02:58,479 --> 00:03:02,660
Pre-Plan explicitly says that no
pre-incident plan has been recorded.

53
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:05,750
Files says that no files
have been attached.

54
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,800
Check each message rather than
assuming a tab contains material

55
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,220
because its name is present.

56
00:03:11,739 --> 00:03:15,929
Calls opens Related calls, covering
calls linked to this contact and

57
00:03:15,929 --> 00:03:17,730
calls at its linked occupancies.

58
00:03:18,069 --> 00:03:20,579
The example returns No related calls.

59
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:24,980
That result describes this contact
query; it does not mean the dispatch

60
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:27,020
console has no active incidents.

61
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:28,989
Settings is lower in the menu.

62
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:33,359
App Info identifies the app,
version, and environment, which

63
00:03:33,359 --> 00:03:34,890
helps when requesting support.

64
00:03:35,309 --> 00:03:39,569
Scrolling reveals Preferences,
including Theme, Language, and Keep

65
00:03:39,569 --> 00:03:41,790
Alive, followed by Support links.

66
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,450
We leave those settings and links
unchanged; this browser view

67
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:48,820
does not prove a device wake
lock or native hardware behavior.

68
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:53,460
Apply the same map, point, and
contact checks to fire and EMS,

69
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:58,630
community-care or mental-health teams,
search and rescue, security, wildland

70
00:03:58,630 --> 00:04:00,850
contractors, and industrial teams.

71
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:05,119
The common task is finding the right
place and recognizing which reference

72
00:04:05,119 --> 00:04:07,069
information is actually available.

